WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.889 --> 00:00:15.980
Thank you for coming tonight. Welcome to the Simons Foundation Presents panel

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:20.779
discussion featuring writers and editors from Quanta Magazine.

00:00:20.779 --> 00:00:28.790
I'm Thomas Lin; I'm the editor of the magazine, and tonight we have a lot of

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:34.190
interesting things to talk about. We will be discussing some of the new ideas and

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:39.110
emerging themes in physics, biology and mathematics, some of the same topics

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:43.310
covered in the magazine and that are also presented in our two new books:

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:48.829
Alice and Bob Meet the Wall of Fire and The Prime Number Conspiracy. One quick

00:00:48.829 --> 00:00:53.420
note about the video you just saw — we will be saving audience questions for

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:57.140
the end and will be bringing microphones to the aisles, so you don't need to use

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:03.200
the speakers in front of you on the chairs. I want to thank the Museum of

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:08.119
Mathematics for being here tonight to help make the books available to all of

00:01:08.119 --> 00:01:14.200
you at this event, and also to MIT Press for working with us to publish the books.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:20.630
Tonight, as those of you who read Quanta are probably familiar with, we will be

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:26.750
talking about some fairly profound ideas and somewhat highly technical — somewhat

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:32.299
technical ideas — but we will, again like the magazine, try to make it as

00:01:32.299 --> 00:01:37.400
accessible and as jargon free as possible. 
It is a great pleasure to

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:44.030
introduce our four panelists tonight. To my left is Natalie Wolchover; she is the

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:50.119
senior physics writer and editor at Quanta. She has been winning lots of awards —

00:01:50.119 --> 00:01:56.600
which are all well deserved. In 2016 she won both the Excellence in Statistical

00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:00.110
Reporting Award and the Everett Clark/Seth Payne Award for Young Science

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:03.979
Journalists, and just last year she won the prestigious American Institute of

00:02:03.979 --> 00:02:09.369
Physics Science Communication Award. Next to her is John Rennie, our deputy editor,

00:02:09.369 --> 00:02:15.140
who leads our biology coverage. He is the former editor in chief of Scientific

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:20.840
American; he was also the host of a TV series on the Weather Channel called

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:25.860
Hacking the Planet. Next to him is Kevin Hartnett, our senior

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:31.470
math writer, and he has had several articles republished in the annual Best

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:37.380
Writing on Mathematics anthology. Last but not least we have Robbert Dijkgraaf.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:43.320
He’s a mathematical physicist and a Quanta columnist. He is the director of the

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:47.820
Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton, New Jersey, and he's also a

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:52.170
painter and extraordinary science communicator. So I'm glad to have all of

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:56.580
you here. Thank you for coming and thank you all for being here tonight, both the

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:01.620
live audience and those watching on the live feed online. Now we don't have a lot

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:06.090
of time here, we only have an hour, and we have a whole universe of ideas to

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:10.530
talk about. And so we're going to just jump right in and start with the first

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:15.420
question, which comes from our science collection, Alice and Bob Meet

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:19.560
the Wall of Fire. The first question in the book asks: Why doesn't our universe

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:27.120
make sense? Many of you are probably aware — and in 2012 there was a major

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:31.740
discovery in physics: The Higgs boson was detected at the Large Hadron Collider in

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:38.790
Switzerland. But physicists were hoping to also detect some other particles as

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:43.350
well that would help them make sense of our universe. Those particles have not

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:50.460
been detected, which leads to questions of whether our universe is natural — which

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:56.250
I'll ask Natalie about in a second — and also to this fear that physicists have had

00:03:56.250 --> 00:04:00.180
for a number of years that if they don't detect these particles, that they will

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:07.110
Natalie — if you could start off by just setting this up for us: What do physicists mean

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:11.040
Natalie, if you could start off by just
setting this up for us what do physicists mean

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:16.770
by the universe being natural or unnatural, and what is it that

00:04:16.770 --> 00:04:24.600
is behind this nightmare scenario? Yeah, so I think a lot of people, when they

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:30.360
hear the idea that the universe is unnatural, that just inherently seems

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:35.880
like an oxymoron. And so over the past five years I've kind of — hopefully — gotten

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:39.140
better at trying to explain what this means.

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:48.390
And so what naturalness means in a scientific sense, is basically

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:55.860
that the variables in the equation can take different values. And the values

00:04:55.860 --> 00:05:00.960
themselves of different constants and things in the equations should all kind

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:04.830
of work together in a way that makes sense. And there's a more precise

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:11.700
way of thinking about it, but with the Higgs boson mass, the problem is that

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:17.940
this particle — the Higgs boson — has a mass which is many, many, many orders of

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:24.000
magnitude different from what’s seen as the fundamental mass in nature.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:31.170
And so this enormous hierarchy between the two values basically seems to

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:35.760
physicists like something that needs to be explained. And they had this brilliant

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:40.320
explanation, which was supersymmetry, that has been sort of the paradigm for a long

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:46.410
time, even before supersymmetry was even searched for.

00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:51.630
People just kind of believed that it was true, and that you would just find the

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:56.310
particles because it was such a beautiful way to make the constants all

00:05:56.310 --> 00:06:01.140
kind of work out together. But instead they did not find these supersymmetric

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:06.870
particles at the Large Hadron Collider. And that's really created a fascinating

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:13.830
time in physics, where this kind of paradigm and principle of naturalness

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:18.720
that was leading people to build the Large Hadron Collider to look for these

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:25.860
particles — that all basically failed, and now physicists don't know how to

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:32.070
make the equations natural. And they also don't even know if this naturalness idea

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:38.610
is even — was a principle they should have been holding in the first place. Another way to think about it,

00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:44.250
I think, is that much of what we have now in terms of some of our bedrock theories

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:48.450
in physics came from the idea that the universe would be natural, right? And yet

00:06:48.450 --> 00:06:52.229
now, if in fact these variables or constants that you're

00:06:52.229 --> 00:06:57.870
talking about are in essence fine-tuned, then it almost leads you to believe that

00:06:57.870 --> 00:07:02.669
our universe is unnatural, and maybe just one of many universes out there. And so

00:07:02.669 --> 00:07:06.330
talk a little bit about what that means — if there's actually a multiverse, and

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:14.180
do physicists really like that idea? Yeah, so, exactly, if — if the equation,

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:19.530
constants in the equations, kind of all make perfect sense with each other then

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:24.870
you can imagine that our universe is this kind of inevitable structure where

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:29.789
everything is kind of cohesive, and there's some underlying kind of theory

00:07:29.789 --> 00:07:37.740
that — that predicts everything based on, you know, pi and two and, you know, maybe

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:42.090
a factor of four somewhere, but very simple kind of math that underlies everything.

00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:48.240
But if you have these really weirdly, you know — and when I say the constants are

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:53.460
weird I mean like one is something like a billion billion billion billion

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:58.440
times bigger than another constant in the equations, and so there's this huge

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:04.650
hierarchy. The only — a way to get that is if everything is just kind of

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:13.759
random in our universe and the constants — there was some sort of miraculous — almost

00:08:13.759 --> 00:08:19.080
really, really unlikely fine-tuning, where, you know, the Higgs mass was really tiny

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:23.310
even though the fundamental mass is really huge, and that difference

00:08:23.310 --> 00:08:30.090
allowed our universe to give rise to life, and so that’s why — Other universes would have a

00:08:30.090 --> 00:08:36.539
different Higgs mass — and in order to have that kind of very unlikely event, then

00:08:36.539 --> 00:08:40.529
you have to have a huge variety of other universes in order for ours to be

00:08:40.529 --> 00:08:43.800
realized. So that's where the multiverse idea kind of

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:52.770
comes in, but it's — people hate that idea because it's untestable — Let’s take a poll — who hates the idea?

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:58.350
— who hates the idea? I mean it's kind of like people who have

00:08:58.350 --> 00:09:01.980
already acclimated to the idea of the multiverse, so, like, this makes perfect

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:06.000
sense. There's many, many universes, why should our

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:10.440
universe be the only one? Why should — why should there just be one universe that

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:17.310
happens to have life? And not a — people who are against the multiverse think

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:20.070
that it's untestable, and that this is kind of a cop-out,

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:24.750
saying, well, we can't explain this big hierarchy so let's just say that it's

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:30.270
all random and there's no explanation. So I want to also ask Robbert about this

00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:34.290
since he's the director of the Institute for Advanced Study. Some of the

00:09:34.290 --> 00:09:38.130
researchers at the institute are studying this exact problem and — and I

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:42.750
wonder if, you know — I want to get your perspective on whether there's a sense

00:09:42.750 --> 00:09:47.670
or — that the universe is in fact natural and we just need to find the right theory — or

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:51.780
if things are trending toward sort of acceptance that it's unnatural, and

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:54.930
sort of buying into in thinking about how the multiverse idea might help

00:09:54.930 --> 00:10:01.770
us. First of all, all my opinions are, here — are just my own, [LAUGHTER] I’m not speaking on behalf of others, because it’s

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:06.450
actually something very much in debate. And I think actually this is kind of

00:10:06.450 --> 00:10:11.850
wonderful expression where Feynman explains how particle physics developed

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:15.690
in his lifetime. He says, no, we have this great beauty — everything fits, and then

00:10:15.690 --> 00:10:19.980
there are little experiments and they do not quite fit. And you try to hold

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:21.750
things together — at some point you have to give up.

00:10:21.750 --> 00:10:27.780
It looks very ugly, and then after a very confusing period it starts to hang

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:32.790
together again, and it's beautiful, but it's beautiful in a more subtle, more deeper

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:37.830
sense. So we know, we go through these transitions. So I feel particle physics

00:10:37.830 --> 00:10:42.240
is very much at that point, because there was an obvious scenario with the Higgs

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:45.840
particle where indeed, you know, certain other particles would be found and

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:50.250
everything would fit together. They do not. But you know, it's no

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:54.450
clear that we have to switch to the other frame and say, well, it's all chaos,

00:10:54.450 --> 00:11:00.090
it's all random etc. So I think we are really in this kind of mixed state. But I

00:11:00.090 --> 00:11:04.470
think an important question we should realize even about the Standard Model, as

00:11:04.470 --> 00:11:09.120
it is: There are lots of numbers that appear there and just — you know — even in

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:13.290
the masses of the particles that we all know, there are huge differences, you know.

00:11:13.290 --> 00:11:17.520
They differ as much as a whale compared to a fly.

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:23.730
And none of us have any idea whether these are real constants of nature or

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:28.950
are they determined by some other set of equations. Are they the solutions of

00:11:28.950 --> 00:11:34.340
equations, or are they constants of nature that somehow nature picked out

00:11:34.340 --> 00:11:41.640
for once and for all? We simply don't know. But if you are — if you are theoretical

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:45.480
physicists and you write down any equation that should describe the

00:11:45.480 --> 00:11:49.190
universe — and it could be a kind of cartoon version of the equations we have —

00:11:49.190 --> 00:11:54.060
it very easily happens that you create more than one universe, right? Because in

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:58.650
some sense it's — if you think about it we almost are saying that, you know, if there

00:11:58.650 --> 00:12:04.230
is a unique universe we are searching for laws of

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:08.640
nature to describe physics or science in terms of you have an input, then

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:14.190
something happens and we calculate the output. But we even are trying to

00:12:14.190 --> 00:12:20.430
determine there's only one solution, and that solution is us. So that's — that's

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:24.360
rare I don't know if any equation in
science that has only one solution you

00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:28.500
know of any equation in science that has only one solution. You could have a

00:12:28.500 --> 00:12:32.310
little potential or something — so think of it as a mountain landscape, but very

00:12:32.310 --> 00:12:36.270
simple — but only one valley. It's very easy — there’s a little bump, and there's a second

00:12:36.270 --> 00:12:41.430
valley, and then what happens to that solution? Right, right. So that's something that, you

00:12:41.430 --> 00:12:46.410
know, I think cosmology confronts physics with. And I think, you know, it's a

00:12:46.410 --> 00:12:51.270
different set of questions because we are not describing what’s often said — you

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:55.950
know, science is about answering the question “What's next?” But it's really, you

00:12:55.950 --> 00:12:59.610
know, why this whole thing? And I think that's actually where we're struggling

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:06.690
with it. I think it's very exciting to see that although all of us hate, I think,

00:13:06.690 --> 00:13:11.220
the multiverse — in the sense, you know, it's free for all — we do know that we had

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:16.890
a whole sequence over many centuries where our sphere of what we think of as

00:13:16.890 --> 00:13:21.690
the world, the universe, has been growing — from our planets to our solar system to

00:13:21.690 --> 00:13:27.180
our galaxy, so there are these increasing — and we feel entirely

00:13:27.180 --> 00:13:31.350
comfortable talking about, perhaps, galaxies

00:13:31.350 --> 00:13:35.220
that we will never see, that are outside the Hubble radius. That's simply,

00:13:35.220 --> 00:13:39.950
you know, because of the expanding universe we will not be able to ever witness. Right. So in some sense

00:13:39.950 --> 00:13:43.560
it's almost as though —  So we feel comfortable with that. I think, you know,

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:45.960
there are things I feel quite comfortable — something happening, you know, a

00:13:45.960 --> 00:13:52.290
few blocks away. I can't observe it but probably it's out there — So in some sense it’s — in thinking only

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:56.670
of our own universe —  We might be provincial. We might be trying to do

00:13:56.670 --> 00:14:00.420
something — and so I think we should always be aware of this feeling that

00:14:00.420 --> 00:14:04.050
Feynman expressed, that, you know, you feel, well, it's going in the wrong

00:14:04.050 --> 00:14:07.950
direction. But what you're doing is something — also like tunneling

00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:13.290
through a barrier — and you might end up in a new valley that's much greener than

00:14:13.290 --> 00:14:17.550
the one that we are in right now. So this leads very well to the next

00:14:17.550 --> 00:14:22.350
question I have which, you know — when you think about the multiverse idea, you

00:14:22.350 --> 00:14:27.240
think about things like string theory. These are all ideas that are

00:14:27.240 --> 00:14:33.450
powerful tools and ways for thinking about our universe, but there's really no

00:14:33.450 --> 00:14:37.550
empirical evidence for these theories. And so I want to talk a little bit about

00:14:37.550 --> 00:14:42.800
what we mean by evidence in science.

00:14:43.190 --> 00:14:51.690
Natalie, in 2015 you covered a debate in Munich just about this question of, you

00:14:51.690 --> 00:14:55.860
know, given that it's getting harder and harder and may not even be possible to

00:14:55.860 --> 00:15:00.930
find empirical evidence for some of the — these most sort of exciting theories

00:15:00.930 --> 00:15:04.560
that are happening at the forefront of physics right now, what exactly does it

00:15:04.560 --> 00:15:12.120
mean to — what counts as evidence in that case — you know, is something that's

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:16.380
mathematical enough? Do we want to follow sort of the

00:15:16.380 --> 00:15:21.630
Karl Popper idea that something has to be falsifiable in this way, which a lot

00:15:21.630 --> 00:15:25.080
of these ideas that we've been talking about today are not. Talk to us a little

00:15:25.080 --> 00:15:29.640
bit about what were some of the key arguments that you covered during that

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:37.530
debate. It was a really amazing opportunity to go to this conference in

00:15:37.530 --> 00:15:44.640
Munich and kind of have this really big theme of physics right now come to a

00:15:44.640 --> 00:15:49.620
climactic point — where you had people from both sides coming together and

00:15:49.620 --> 00:15:54.510
basically arguing about whether string theory and the multiverse and these

00:15:54.510 --> 00:15:59.850
ideas that are not directly testable are scientific, or whether science is kind of

00:15:59.850 --> 00:16:06.660
going off course by focusing on these kinds of theories. And one big — I think

00:16:06.660 --> 00:16:10.860
one big theme that emerged is there — there's so much anger and passion on

00:16:10.860 --> 00:16:17.089
both sides — and that is usually a mark of confusion and a confusing time

00:16:17.089 --> 00:16:25.260
where things are subject to change rapidly. And I think, what was kind of —

00:16:25.260 --> 00:16:30.570
one thing that was clear, is that — it's not really about physicists kind of

00:16:30.570 --> 00:16:37.980
going down the wrong path and becoming more interested in speculative theories

00:16:37.980 --> 00:16:43.440
when they should be more grounded. It's really that we as a humanity, we've kind

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:51.420
of gotten to the ends of the points where we can really push further. Like we —

00:16:51.420 --> 00:16:56.790
we can no longer build instruments that can probe to a much smaller scale than we

00:16:56.790 --> 00:17:00.990
have; we can see out to the cosmic horizon but we'll never see further than

00:17:00.990 --> 00:17:08.280
that. So we're kind of coming to the end of where we can actually test our ideas.

00:17:08.280 --> 00:17:14.610
But theorists have, of course, because of just logical kind of thinking about how

00:17:14.610 --> 00:17:17.850
how things would extend, have gone much further in both directions. So they're

00:17:17.850 --> 00:17:20.939
thinking about the multiverse on large scales, and they're thinking about

00:17:20.939 --> 00:17:26.610
strings and string theory on the small scales, even though we'll never see

00:17:26.610 --> 00:17:32.460
that far down. So a big part of the debate is whether there are other kinds

00:17:32.460 --> 00:17:36.530
of evidence besides direct empirical evidence. Some people think it's just

00:17:36.530 --> 00:17:41.580
blasphemy to even suggest that there might be ways of kind of gaining

00:17:41.580 --> 00:17:46.320
confidence in a theory that is not having to do with experiments and

00:17:46.320 --> 00:17:52.020
testing the theory. But other people think that just by looking at the logic

00:17:52.020 --> 00:17:57.750
of an idea you can gain confidence in it. And so the big question is, with string

00:17:57.750 --> 00:18:00.489
theory — are physicists just kind of fooling

00:18:00.489 --> 00:18:06.279
themselves or dazzled by the beauty of it, or are they seeing real kind of

00:18:06.279 --> 00:18:10.989
qualities in string theory that seem compelling enough to actually believe it.

00:18:10.989 --> 00:18:16.210
We're going to come back to this question in a bit. Thanks for setting up that

00:18:16.210 --> 00:18:23.919
debate for us. You know, thinking about evidence, in the realm of physics there

00:18:23.919 --> 00:18:30.309
are clear examples of very testable ideas and experiments and — and empirical

00:18:30.309 --> 00:18:35.859
evidence for what we know in physics. But there are, as Natalie was saying, many

00:18:35.859 --> 00:18:40.779
newer theories that rely more on mathematics to sort of prove that at

00:18:40.779 --> 00:18:46.779
least it's something that’s logically consistent. Kevin, in the world of pure

00:18:46.779 --> 00:18:53.950
mathematics, which does not rely on the physical world and deals with, you

00:18:53.950 --> 00:19:01.899
know, abstractions, where evidence is not inherently empirical, what is evidence in

00:19:01.899 --> 00:19:06.309
mathematics, what counts as evidence?Sure, I mean what counts as knowledge is

00:19:06.309 --> 00:19:12.249
a proof, and short of that, you know, kind of what matters — we ran an article in

00:19:12.249 --> 00:19:17.019
Quanta a couple weeks ago on this concept of the rank of elliptic curves

00:19:17.019 --> 00:19:20.739
and whether or not the rank is bounded: Is there a cap on how big it can be?

00:19:20.739 --> 00:19:24.429
There has been this — kind of this — these sea changes over the last century.

00:19:24.429 --> 00:19:28.629
A hundred years ago people thought for sure it had to be a cap, and in the ’60s

00:19:28.629 --> 00:19:33.340
and ’70s they thought, no way, it must be unbounded. And now they're kind of

00:19:33.340 --> 00:19:36.879
— opinion has shifted back toward there being a cap. But kind of, why? Right? No one

00:19:36.879 --> 00:19:40.090
has proven it one way or the other, or even really come that close. And so I

00:19:40.090 --> 00:19:42.789
think they're kind of — there are different types of evidence that matter

00:19:42.789 --> 00:19:47.049
and that move opinion. There's computational evidence, and as computers

00:19:47.049 --> 00:19:53.320
get better you can do more computations. And if every time you find a solution to

00:19:53.320 --> 00:19:57.279
the Riemann zeta-function, it lies on the critical strip that makes you think

00:19:57.279 --> 00:20:01.690
every solution lies on this on — every zero of the function lies on this strip.

00:20:01.690 --> 00:20:05.529
So there's computational evidence. There are heuristics — if you kind of compare

00:20:05.529 --> 00:20:09.460
something — if you're interested in understanding a property of Object A and

00:20:09.460 --> 00:20:14.710
you can't prove that property, you know, but you know that Object A is

00:20:14.710 --> 00:20:18.940
kind of like Object B, and Object B has this analogous property, and we can prove

00:20:18.940 --> 00:20:23.080
that the property holds for B — that's again heuristic. That lets us think that

00:20:23.080 --> 00:20:25.659
the property also holds for A. And you see that happening

00:20:25.659 --> 00:20:30.549
sometimes in mathematics, and physics often provides a kind of a source of

00:20:30.549 --> 00:20:34.960
evidence for mathematical things being true. I know in the collection Robbert has

00:20:34.960 --> 00:20:39.970
a column that talks about mirror symmetry, and mirror symmetry is

00:20:39.970 --> 00:20:44.679
something we've covered in Quanta as well — so physicists kind of proposed that

00:20:44.679 --> 00:20:49.659
there are these two models of string theory that seemed equivalent, and when

00:20:49.659 --> 00:20:52.210
you think about those models in mathematics, it tells you that these two

00:20:52.210 --> 00:20:56.740
types of geometric spaces are equivalent — these symplectic spaces and these complex

00:20:56.740 --> 00:21:01.030
spaces. So physics kind of proposes that their physical theories work if these

00:21:01.030 --> 00:21:03.909
two types of spaces are equivalent. And then mathematically you might then go

00:21:03.909 --> 00:21:07.780
out and actually try to prove that these two spaces are equivalent. So that's kind of a source of

00:21:07.780 --> 00:21:11.200
evidence in math, and I guess I would say, what is the upshot here? Because, you know,

00:21:11.200 --> 00:21:15.580
evidence is not a proof. And evidence can in some ways — often doesn't lead you

00:21:15.580 --> 00:21:18.970
at all closer to a proof than you were before. But I think the main thing it

00:21:18.970 --> 00:21:23.770
does is it kind of emboldens mathematicians to pursue lines of

00:21:23.770 --> 00:21:29.140
inquiry. I talked to a mathematician once who told me that he'd been working on

00:21:29.140 --> 00:21:32.700
this problem for a long time and then he heard that some other mathematician

00:21:32.700 --> 00:21:36.730
proved — you know, proved the conjecture was true, and he didn't know what they

00:21:36.730 --> 00:21:40.570
did to prove it was true. But just knowing that it had been proven true, like, you

00:21:40.570 --> 00:21:45.400
know, the next day he figured out his own proof [LAUGHTER], because he just suddenly had this new faith, that it

00:21:45.400 --> 00:21:49.720
probably worked. So I think that is kind of a major — that's something that

00:21:49.720 --> 00:21:54.010
evidence can do in mathematics. That's great. Thank you, Kevin.

00:21:54.010 --> 00:21:59.710
You know, we live in a time right now where I think evidence — at least arguably

00:21:59.710 --> 00:22:06.190
evidence — and facts are almost existential issues for our democratic

00:22:06.190 --> 00:22:11.650
nation. And, but you know, I wanted to ask Robbert — as a mathematical physicist,

00:22:11.650 --> 00:22:17.650
because you work sort of in both worlds — to put nonempirical evidence into

00:22:17.650 --> 00:22:23.530
context for us: When is it useful, how does it help us move science and

00:22:23.530 --> 00:22:27.340
mathematics forward when we cannot rely on evidence from the

00:22:27.340 --> 00:22:32.530
physical world? Well, by the way, your — your anecdotes suggest that you should

00:22:32.530 --> 00:22:36.520
have fake news outs [LAUGHTER] that the famous conjecture was proven, because then

00:22:36.520 --> 00:22:44.560
somebody might feel, oh, I can do this, too. Like if we didn't — if we don't have

00:22:44.560 --> 00:22:47.110
empirical evidence for — for, say, a physics theory,

00:22:47.110 --> 00:22:50.230
can we just then say: I like this theory better? Even in pure math, there is kind

00:22:55.300 --> 00:23:00.310
of almost the art form of the conjecture, right? So what makes a good conjecture?

00:23:00.310 --> 00:23:04.840
It's by definition something that hasn't been proven. You have some suggested

00:23:04.840 --> 00:23:10.150
evidence, perhaps some cases, etc. — so I think both the math and physics — kind of

00:23:10.150 --> 00:23:15.250
the dotted line where we want to go — is as important as, actually, you know, taking

00:23:15.250 --> 00:23:19.650
the steps and proving the points. In fact, I think, you know, it's these more visionary

00:23:19.650 --> 00:23:23.980
elements both in math and physics that are motivating us. Why are you working on

00:23:23.980 --> 00:23:28.240
a certain problem? Because it's not just one step further, but it's a step along a

00:23:28.240 --> 00:23:32.470
route that will take you to a completely different world. So in some sense you

00:23:32.470 --> 00:23:38.650
have to make sense of a few facts. If you're a number theorist, you know, perhaps

00:23:38.650 --> 00:23:42.850
you can work on concrete cases. If you are a theoretical physicist,

00:23:42.850 --> 00:23:48.850
now nature has given us a few hints that the world is not yet finished. So if you

00:23:48.850 --> 00:23:52.930
think of the standard model, there are things like neutrino masses, there's the

00:23:52.930 --> 00:23:57.940
Higgs particle that doesn't really quite work, you know, there is the difference

00:23:57.940 --> 00:24:04.080
between particles and antiparticles so
you somehow know this is this has to

00:24:04.080 --> 00:24:09.670
nature has found a solution to it, and I think it's that kind of soft thinking —

00:24:09.670 --> 00:24:13.720
which is incredibly important

00:24:13.720 --> 00:24:20.050
because it actually guides our thoughts. And you know there's a long tradition of, you know, theoretical physicists getting things wrong, but there's also a long

00:24:20.050 --> 00:24:26.440
tradition of, you know, theoretical physicists getting things wrong, but

00:24:26.440 --> 00:24:31.330
there's also a long tradition of somehow being — that their theories are smarter

00:24:31.330 --> 00:24:36.610
than the person who proposed it, that they're holding back. Now Einstein is

00:24:36.610 --> 00:24:40.390
famous for many of the consequences of general relativity — he felt uncomfortable

00:24:40.390 --> 00:24:47.080
with, you know, Dirac basically on paper predicting antiparticles, but nobody

00:24:47.080 --> 00:24:53.110
had seen these particles, so you held back. So I think it's very important

00:24:53.110 --> 00:24:58.270
that we also, you know, encourage guided imagination: guided with previous

00:24:58.270 --> 00:25:04.300
examples, guided with previous experiments, etc., push theorists, because

00:25:04.300 --> 00:25:08.620
you can also make the mistake —somebody said in one of the discussions, who

00:25:08.620 --> 00:25:12.370
needs the thought police? And I think indeed — in thinking we don't need

00:25:12.370 --> 00:25:17.770
policing, we just need smart thinking. And it should be based on sometimes what we

00:25:17.770 --> 00:25:24.490
call intuition. But I think intuition is our way which by experience we learn to

00:25:24.490 --> 00:25:30.520
deal with kind of lack of data, error bars in our thinking, and trying to find

00:25:30.520 --> 00:25:33.940
whether there is something that, you know — can you kind of fit in some sense a

00:25:33.940 --> 00:25:39.130
curve through this, and that's — so part of theory should also be that you're out in

00:25:39.130 --> 00:25:44.440
front of the data and guide — and it's actually quite literally what we need.

00:25:44.440 --> 00:25:48.460
Because in modern experiments people don't realize that when they create

00:25:48.460 --> 00:25:55.840
billions of collisions a second, but the machine can only keep, so to say, a few. So

00:25:55.840 --> 00:26:01.210
you — even the experimenter, the one thing they want to know is what should we look

00:26:01.210 --> 00:26:01.710
for, because they can't look for everything at the same time. So in a way

00:26:04.510 --> 00:26:08.020
you're speaking to this dynamic between theory and experiments. And sometimes the

00:26:08.020 --> 00:26:12.220
experiments give rise to new theories, so as you need theory to

00:26:12.220 --> 00:26:15.700
know what to look for with your experiment — You need both. So in that sense, I

00:26:15.700 --> 00:26:18.730
think if you have a diverse community where people are searching in many

00:26:18.730 --> 00:26:23.320
different directions, that's good. If we are all, like, on one little cluster thinking

00:26:23.320 --> 00:26:27.760
the same way and we all say yes to this
or no to that I think that would be very

00:26:27.760 --> 00:26:33.340
the same way, and we all say yes to this or no to that, I think that would be very dangerous. Thank you. Now I want to turn to the idea of biological evidence.

00:26:33.340 --> 00:26:36.820
Because, you know, in physics — at least in theoretical particle physics —we're sort

00:26:36.820 --> 00:26:40.780
of — it seems like we're kind of hitting a wall in some ways. But with biology it's,

00:26:40.780 --> 00:26:45.070
you know, biology — biologists, I think in general, insist on empirical evidence,

00:26:45.070 --> 00:26:50.290
right? And yet biology is a very complex subject, also. The systems are

00:26:50.290 --> 00:26:53.710
extremely complex. So it's not necessarily easy to make

00:26:53.710 --> 00:26:56.710
sense of the data that you have, and make
makes sense of the evidence that you're

00:26:56.710 --> 00:27:00.460
collecting. And also, if you're thinking about things like evolution, and studying

00:27:00.460 --> 00:27:05.470
how evolution works, obviously — well, not obviously to everyone, but I think to people who

00:27:05.470 --> 00:27:09.370
care about evidence, obviously — there’s an overwhelming amount of evidence for

00:27:09.370 --> 00:27:13.120
evolution itself. There's no question that evolution itself occurred, but there

00:27:13.120 --> 00:27:18.430
are questions about specific mechanisms by which it works. And thinking

00:27:18.430 --> 00:27:21.880
back — obviously we can't go back in time to see exactly what happened — so

00:27:21.880 --> 00:27:26.070
there's all sorts of challenges to understanding biological evidence.

00:27:26.070 --> 00:27:32.310
John, I'd love for you to talk a little bit about what new kinds of data and

00:27:32.310 --> 00:27:37.240
evidence we're gaining in biology to help us better understand things like

00:27:37.240 --> 00:27:45.280
evolution and what life is. Sure, so obviously all kinds of information

00:27:45.280 --> 00:27:48.250
available to us can find a way of working a way into different sorts of

00:27:48.250 --> 00:27:51.880
biology, and biology is many different things in many different situations, just

00:27:51.880 --> 00:27:56.440
as life itself is. I feel like biology in some ways is faced with a similar sort

00:27:56.440 --> 00:28:01.810
of problem to the ones you were talking about with physics, in — in that we in —

00:28:01.810 --> 00:28:05.710
in the biology world, there's also a certain sort of level of tension because our

00:28:05.710 --> 00:28:10.000
understanding of life as being a result of evolution is that there's obviously a

00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:13.840
huge element of randomness that is the germ of the — the source of all the

00:28:13.840 --> 00:28:17.590
variation on which life begins. But then other things that are applying much more

00:28:17.590 --> 00:28:23.410
regularity to then lead us to certain sorts of ends. So contingency is

00:28:23.410 --> 00:28:26.920
obviously very important in our understanding of biology, in the history

00:28:26.920 --> 00:28:32.170
of life on Earth and anyplace else. But what can be intellectually unsatisfying

00:28:32.170 --> 00:28:34.990
about that is that you don't want us to just think that life is just a result of

00:28:34.990 --> 00:28:38.770
some random thing that just happened. We would like to think that we understand

00:28:38.770 --> 00:28:42.760
certain sorts of principles that made certain kinds of things more likely than

00:28:42.760 --> 00:28:45.640
others. So there's a certain kind of balance that we're trying to go to with

00:28:45.640 --> 00:28:50.710
that. And I think where that's surfaced a lot in — in a couple of — the two ways that

00:28:50.710 --> 00:28:54.940
I think strike me as really particularly fascinating these days, and would drive a

00:28:54.940 --> 00:28:59.500
lot of the biology: One is that our traditional view of life is one that's

00:28:59.500 --> 00:29:02.620
based on sort of being something kind of like what we are. You know, we would talk

00:29:02.620 --> 00:29:05.200
about — there is no universally agreed-upon

00:29:05.200 --> 00:29:07.840
definition for life. We talk about it as being sort of like a

00:29:07.840 --> 00:29:12.700
thermodynamically open system; we talk about its being made of cells; we

00:29:12.700 --> 00:29:17.110
talk about it having like a property of homeostasis, and that these life

00:29:17.110 --> 00:29:23.470
reproduces and so forth, but what's interesting is that when

00:29:23.470 --> 00:29:27.549
we've been learning more about life and the diversity of life, what we're seeing

00:29:27.549 --> 00:29:31.630
is that a lot of the things that we thought of as sort of the mainstream — the

00:29:31.630 --> 00:29:36.850
stuff like us — but we are weirdly more of an edge case than we thought. And that a

00:29:36.850 --> 00:29:40.450
lot of the things that we thought were the edge cases are much more mainstream.

00:29:40.450 --> 00:29:44.500
So — viruses. Everybody — whoever has that sort of sense of, oh life is made of — you

00:29:44.500 --> 00:29:48.250
know, life is independent and it perpetuates itself and it's made of

00:29:48.250 --> 00:29:51.250
cells — you would look at viruses and you would think well, they're a weird sort of

00:29:51.250 --> 00:29:55.570
parasitic thing. Are they alive? Eh — let's try to move past that. Well, what

00:29:55.570 --> 00:29:59.980
we're discovering is that viruses are in fact a gigantically central part of all

00:29:59.980 --> 00:30:03.789
of life, that there are more viruses and more types of viruses and an abundance

00:30:03.789 --> 00:30:08.139
of — abundance of viruses in nature. That's hugely more important than we ever

00:30:08.139 --> 00:30:11.740
thought. And in fact, that there are viruses that are bigger than some types

00:30:11.740 --> 00:30:14.919
of bacteria. So all the different sorts of things that we were using to sort of

00:30:14.919 --> 00:30:18.850
marginalize some types of biological phenomena because they weren't like us

00:30:18.850 --> 00:30:22.419
turn out to be much more important than we thought. So one thing is that

00:30:22.419 --> 00:30:25.630
in that search search for the right sort of evidence — we have to pay more attention

00:30:25.630 --> 00:30:29.950
to those things that we didn't think were important. The other thing is that

00:30:29.950 --> 00:30:34.330
we've traditionally thought of — certainly Post-Darwin — that we have tended

00:30:34.330 --> 00:30:42.820
to think that we needed to really look to evolution as being the

00:30:42.820 --> 00:30:46.659
source of all of the order that we would find in life. But what we're increasingly

00:30:46.659 --> 00:30:50.080
finding, and what is a fascinating area of research that I'm lucky that we

00:30:50.080 --> 00:30:53.889
actually get to write about so much at Quanta, is the way in which life can be

00:30:53.889 --> 00:30:57.850
treated as a mathematical system or a physical system, and that a lot of the

00:30:57.850 --> 00:31:02.950
sources of order that we see in the living world are already basically there

00:31:02.950 --> 00:31:08.019
to be taken advantage of within physical systems anyway, and that life didn't have

00:31:08.019 --> 00:31:12.279
to evolve certain sorts of systems. So the fact that cells, the sort of default

00:31:12.279 --> 00:31:16.570
shape of a cell is being round, it's not round because it was evolved to be round.

00:31:16.570 --> 00:31:20.630
It's round because a sphere is the natural minimal shape of

00:31:20.630 --> 00:31:24.590
being a way to contain something inside a membrane. And we're finding more

00:31:24.590 --> 00:31:30.770
and more that some kinds of physical phenomena with increasing levels of

00:31:30.770 --> 00:31:35.990
sophistication can be sources of vitally important structure and functions in

00:31:35.990 --> 00:31:41.539
biology, and that's a huge frontier for us.
Right. It's certainly been a recent rise in

00:31:41.539 --> 00:31:47.150
in the power of biophysics and trying to merge things from these, you know, sort of — kind of

00:31:47.150 --> 00:31:51.289
from, you know, the small from physics in terms, you know, at a more basic

00:31:51.289 --> 00:31:55.820
particle level to the sort of larger, more emergent state that — that biology

00:31:55.820 --> 00:32:01.190
tends to — to examine. You know, thinking about evolution and life and even

00:32:01.190 --> 00:32:06.289
our own experiences — everything that we experience and think about in terms

00:32:06.289 --> 00:32:12.590
of these things involves a directionality to time, right? That's how

00:32:12.590 --> 00:32:15.799
we experience things. I'm looking up at the clock, you know, every now and then, to

00:32:15.799 --> 00:32:21.110
see, you know, where we are in terms of our time here [LAUGHTER], but in physics the constant of

00:32:21.110 --> 00:32:24.530
time isn't really well defined. The fundamental laws of physics — things can

00:32:24.530 --> 00:32:29.960
kind of move forward or backward in time. What are physicists doing about that

00:32:29.960 --> 00:32:36.280
and, you know is — as I understand it based on your reporting and the story in our

00:32:36.280 --> 00:32:41.570
science collection, is it connected in some way to quantum entanglement? Is that

00:32:41.570 --> 00:32:50.030
helping us understand what time is from a physics perspective? Yes, sort of. So, I

00:32:50.030 --> 00:32:55.280
think there's many different mysteries about time that are all kind of related,

00:32:55.280 --> 00:32:59.150
or maybe they're the same mystery. So there's —there's like time as kind of

00:32:59.150 --> 00:33:05.570
this background, this dimension that we're moving in. And then there's

00:33:05.570 --> 00:33:10.100
the arrow of time, so the fact that there's a directionality of time, but that arrow

00:33:10.100 --> 00:33:15.620
might kind of be on top of the background of existing time. And then we

00:33:15.620 --> 00:33:20.000
also have, like, this perception of time that changes, but then that also — our

00:33:20.000 --> 00:33:23.659
perception might also be more related to actual time than we think. So I feel like

00:33:23.659 --> 00:33:28.070
there's — when people talk about time, it's even hard to understand what the

00:33:28.070 --> 00:33:32.360
question is sometimes. But for sure, it's one of

00:33:32.360 --> 00:33:39.320
the big mysteries of physics. And I think it's sort of — I mean, I would say it's

00:33:39.320 --> 00:33:43.250
maybe the big mystery of physics. I don't know how you feel about that, but I just

00:33:43.250 --> 00:33:49.100
just  —I think it's sort of very, very tied with all the other things that are most

00:33:49.100 --> 00:33:54.860
mysterious. So, like quantum gravity and the nature of the Big Bang and all of

00:33:54.860 --> 00:33:58.880
these things. I think if we knew — if we understood time, we would also

00:33:58.880 --> 00:34:05.929
understand all these other things. But there is — but, yeah, there

00:34:05.929 --> 00:34:13.520
is progress in understanding the arrow of time, so why time can move forward but

00:34:13.520 --> 00:34:18.649
you can't kind of — you know even — we can move in three dimensions of space,

00:34:18.649 --> 00:34:24.980
we can't move backwards in time, and I think that is becoming more and more

00:34:24.980 --> 00:34:29.300
understood as being associated with — with the growth of quantum

00:34:29.300 --> 00:34:35.210
entanglement. So, the growth of things, kind of — all particles, all interacting in

00:34:35.210 --> 00:34:40.460
the universe, and getting more and more, basically, where their own state can't be

00:34:40.460 --> 00:34:44.840
defined individually, their state is very dependent on the states of other things.

00:34:44.840 --> 00:34:50.570
And so information is kind of shared between many, many different particles. So

00:34:50.570 --> 00:34:56.710
this gradual growth of entanglement of particles and stuff in the universe is

00:34:56.710 --> 00:35:01.670
more and more being associated with the flow of time, because it's very hard to

00:35:01.670 --> 00:35:07.790
unentangle — things don't become unentangled over time. But even that, I

00:35:07.790 --> 00:35:14.660
think, is — a lot of people would view that as a separate question from, just, why is

00:35:14.660 --> 00:35:20.510
there this sense — why is everything not happening at one instant, right now? Like

00:35:20.510 --> 00:35:24.859
how do you get this — how is everything unpacked so that you're allowed to have,

00:35:24.859 --> 00:35:30.920
like, particles becoming entangled, more and more entangled. I think that's

00:35:30.920 --> 00:35:37.820
completely unanswered. Sure, Sure. So I do want to get Robbert’s take on this

00:35:37.820 --> 00:35:42.109
as well. I mean — Quite right. It's one of the great paradoxes. So I

00:35:42.109 --> 00:35:46.070
think on one hand we can be quite confident that space and time

00:35:46.070 --> 00:35:50.630
are one and the same thing, so Einstein told us, and everything — all the evidence

00:35:50.630 --> 00:35:54.050
points out that if you understand space and time, you can basically have

00:35:54.050 --> 00:35:58.610
any combination of it. And then on top of that, I think we have much more

00:35:58.610 --> 00:36:03.710
understanding about the kind of, oh let’s say, like the granular nature of space. So we

00:36:03.710 --> 00:36:08.000
know that if you want to study space at the smallest distances, we would have to

00:36:08.000 --> 00:36:12.680
concentrate energy so tightly that a little black hole would form and it

00:36:12.680 --> 00:36:17.030
would basically eat a little hole in the space. So you could never see it at the

00:36:17.030 --> 00:36:23.690
smallest distances, and there are great theoretical models how space can

00:36:23.690 --> 00:36:29.510
appear, emerge out of quantum fluctuations, quantum information, etc. So

00:36:29.510 --> 00:36:34.760
it's, like, not fundamental, something that comes out. So if you take these two

00:36:34.760 --> 00:36:38.840
arguments, you said time should also emerge, it should also — we should have a

00:36:38.840 --> 00:36:42.470
kind of a granular nature of time. It would be terrific because people always

00:36:42.470 --> 00:36:47.390
ask you, what happened at the Big Bang, or before the Big Bang? But if time is

00:36:47.390 --> 00:36:51.350
something like a river, that you go upstream, at some point you will just

00:36:51.350 --> 00:36:55.640
have little drops of water — the whole concept of a river would disappear, and

00:36:55.640 --> 00:37:02.810
you don't have a paradox. Rivers have beginnings. That's perfectly fine. But I

00:37:02.810 --> 00:37:06.380
think obviously nobody has any idea how to make this work. And what we find in

00:37:06.380 --> 00:37:10.520
particular, I think, that quantum mechanics, which often is seen now in

00:37:10.520 --> 00:37:14.450
this battle between general relativity, gravity and quantum mechanics, that like

00:37:14.450 --> 00:37:18.230
like — quantum mechanics has the last word. Because all the theories still that we

00:37:18.230 --> 00:37:22.430
have, like string theory, etc., they use kind of ordinary quantum mechanics.

00:37:22.430 --> 00:37:26.090
Quantum mechanics makes a huge distinction between time and the other

00:37:26.090 --> 00:37:30.260
directions, because again it describes how things evolve in time, whether it's

00:37:30.260 --> 00:37:35.540
a wave function or entanglement, etc. But this can't be the last word, so we kind

00:37:35.540 --> 00:37:39.170
of have to give up. And so I think there's a lot of physicists who think in

00:37:39.170 --> 00:37:44.720
this battle between gravity and quantum, where somehow quantum seems to have won

00:37:44.720 --> 00:37:48.860
the first round in our ideas [LAUGHTER], but in the second round we'll have to give up.

00:37:48.860 --> 00:37:53.840
Because the concept of time is totally conventional in quantum mechanics. And

00:37:53.840 --> 00:37:56.930
as Natalie’s saying, that has to somehow disappear.

00:37:56.930 --> 00:38:00.359
So who knows?
That's actually extremely exciting,

00:38:00.359 --> 00:38:03.359
and it's actually so fascinating. But, of course, because you've all, you know —

00:38:03.359 --> 00:38:07.019
physicists are pushing, you know, and they can’t build a microscope that's

00:38:07.019 --> 00:38:11.819
small enough or telescope that’s big enough to explore — actually time is

00:38:11.819 --> 00:38:15.390
happening right now. It's something that's happening here, we're experiencing

00:38:15.390 --> 00:38:19.650
it. So physics to a large extent is also about the things that just surround us.

00:38:19.650 --> 00:38:24.660
And you can ask very deep questions — we can be totally confused about everyday

00:38:24.660 --> 00:38:29.910
concepts, which is actually humbling, because people have been thinking about

00:38:29.910 --> 00:38:34.319
time for millennia, but it shows that we do not necessarily have to build huge

00:38:34.319 --> 00:38:39.660
experiments. We can just think about it, and — and wonder about it. And the

00:38:39.660 --> 00:38:43.499
biggest puzzles of physics — perhaps time is it — it's actually something that we

00:38:43.499 --> 00:38:48.459
are experiencing right, now, this moment, whatever that sentence means.

00:38:48.459 --> 00:38:51.480
Right. Do you want to add something about time? Just a small thing. I mean I

00:38:51.480 --> 00:38:55.049
love to read the articles about — about time and the rest purely as a kind of,

00:38:55.049 --> 00:38:57.509
like, you know, a tourist going through this area [LAUGHTER] of things, because I'm

00:38:57.509 --> 00:39:00.930
fascinated just to see what comes up in it. But what also really strikes me about

00:39:00.930 --> 00:39:08.099
that all the time — sorry, inevitable. [LAUGHTER] — is the fact that our understanding, our

00:39:08.099 --> 00:39:13.019
appreciation of time this way comes in the fact that we evolved to have some

00:39:13.019 --> 00:39:17.489
kind of experience of time. It was something, for whatever reasons, out of

00:39:17.489 --> 00:39:22.049
necessity, or again contingency, or whatever — we evolved to have an

00:39:22.049 --> 00:39:27.059
appreciation of time that works the way that it does, and it's deeply ingrained

00:39:27.059 --> 00:39:32.489
inside the nature of life itself. I mean if you — if all things down to the

00:39:32.489 --> 00:39:36.329
cells, down to the most primitive level, they all make — they all make use of

00:39:36.329 --> 00:39:40.589
time, they all keep track of time to organize their activities in different

00:39:40.589 --> 00:39:44.940
ways, and I mean it's really fascinating to understand why that is the case, and

00:39:44.940 --> 00:39:48.779
whether there is an option for it to be any different. About, like, whether is

00:39:48.779 --> 00:39:51.869
— is there something about — that we appreciate time because this is the only

00:39:51.869 --> 00:39:56.400
way, that we would have to perceive it in that way? Or, you know, could something be

00:39:56.400 --> 00:40:02.430
like the — the aliens in, I think, Vonnegut’s book Slaughterhouse-Five,

00:40:02.430 --> 00:40:05.730
where it's like the aliens, the Tralfamadorians — there it is — who

00:40:05.730 --> 00:40:10.680
appreciated, they — they experienced all times simultaneously— could something do

00:40:10.680 --> 00:40:13.700
that? Or is — or is there something built into the

00:40:13.700 --> 00:40:17.540
nature of the physics that then in biology has to come out of — that makes

00:40:17.540 --> 00:40:20.930
that impossible? The big question, right: Is time fundamental? Is it emergent?

00:40:20.930 --> 00:40:25.450
Is it just a figment of our imagination? Is it an illusion? This is something that

00:40:25.450 --> 00:40:30.830
physicists and biologists at a different level are very keen to understand,

00:40:30.830 --> 00:40:39.740
and — and philosophers, too. So, getting back to the question of math and evidence, you

00:40:39.740 --> 00:40:43.790
know I wanted us to think a little bit about the question of math as

00:40:43.790 --> 00:40:47.630
the universal language of science. Because certainly a lot of the science that we

00:40:47.630 --> 00:40:55.460
cover at Quanta tends to involve some very rigorous math, and, you know, there's

00:40:55.460 --> 00:41:00.200
a pretty well-known essay from 1960 by the Nobel Prize-winning physicist Eugene

00:41:00.200 --> 00:41:05.600
Wigner in which he talked about the unreasonable effectiveness of

00:41:05.600 --> 00:41:10.160
mathematics. So we all are familiar with math that can come out of physical

00:41:10.160 --> 00:41:13.520
things. We can think of a circle, we think about, you know, pi and the relationship

00:41:13.520 --> 00:41:17.540
between the circumference and diameter. There's a look at motions of — orbits of

00:41:17.540 --> 00:41:21.970
planets, things like that, but there's been a lot of mathematics developed

00:41:21.970 --> 00:41:29.810
separate from what we can observe, and somehow that mathematics — some of it,

00:41:29.810 --> 00:41:35.060
at least — has found its way back into physics and back into descriptions of

00:41:35.060 --> 00:41:38.960
the real world. And so that's, I think, what he was referring to, and it also

00:41:38.960 --> 00:41:43.550
sort of speaks to the — sort of the deep question of whether mathematics is, you

00:41:43.550 --> 00:41:47.600
know, invented or discovered. And some of the mathematicians I've spoken to tend

00:41:47.600 --> 00:41:53.140
to say it's both, right? That both things are happening. But I wanted to ask Robbert

00:41:53.140 --> 00:41:57.650
about this — well, actually, I'm sorry, I'm going to start

00:41:57.650 --> 00:42:03.109
with Kevin first, because Kevin wrote about a strange link between physics and

00:42:03.109 --> 00:42:07.550
math in one of your stories in the science book. Talk a little about that. Right —

00:42:07.550 --> 00:42:11.840
— about particle physics and pure mathematics. Yeah, this was a story about

00:42:11.840 --> 00:42:19.250
a mathematician at Oxford named Francis Brown who was just kind of exploring

00:42:19.250 --> 00:42:21.830
this correspondence between these important numbers in math and these

00:42:21.830 --> 00:42:28.430
important numbers in particle physics. So when particle physicists run — you know

00:42:28.430 --> 00:42:32.510
— collide two particles together, and they want to kind of follow that chain

00:42:32.510 --> 00:42:36.619
reaction forward and try and determine what happens, they use this thing called

00:42:36.619 --> 00:42:41.599
the Feynman diagram. And each Feynman diagram you associate by, say, a

00:42:41.599 --> 00:42:45.710
probability that the — the collision played out kind of the way your diagram

00:42:45.710 --> 00:42:50.240
said it played out. And so you have this number describing these collisions

00:42:50.240 --> 00:42:55.010
called the amplitude, and then there are all these other numbers in pure math that

00:42:55.010 --> 00:42:58.880
are called motives or periods of motives. But the point being, they’re just

00:42:58.880 --> 00:43:04.730
like a characteristic number of algebraic equations, and these — these

00:43:04.730 --> 00:43:09.039
numbers, these probabilities, for these Feynman diagrams seem to kind of

00:43:09.039 --> 00:43:14.930
match these — these periods of motives in this field of algebraic geometry. And so

00:43:14.930 --> 00:43:19.339
Francis Brown, in coordination with physicists — really it's a quite a dynamic

00:43:19.339 --> 00:43:22.849
back and forth they're having — has been trying to explore, like, well, why would

00:43:22.849 --> 00:43:27.049
these — what is — what is the structure of these numbers that we're seeing coming

00:43:27.049 --> 00:43:30.740
out of these physics experiments? Because it does seem like they have a structure,

00:43:30.740 --> 00:43:33.829
they have this thing called a group structure, that mathematicians would be

00:43:33.829 --> 00:43:38.150
interested in understanding. And at the same time, if there is this structure,

00:43:38.150 --> 00:43:44.630
what does that mean physically? Because if these — these periods have, or these

00:43:44.630 --> 00:43:47.630
amplitudes have, this group structure, it's suggesting something symmetrical is

00:43:47.630 --> 00:43:50.900
happening in the way these particle collisions are having, and that symmetry

00:43:50.900 --> 00:43:54.770
should have an actual physical meaning. So it's kind of this dynamic back and

00:43:54.770 --> 00:43:58.880
forth between the two, and I think it's one instance I would just say — I've just

00:43:58.880 --> 00:44:03.829
— I think one of the most exciting things going on in math today is this — I feel,

00:44:03.829 --> 00:44:07.849
like a growing collaboration between number theory and physics. Geometry and

00:44:07.849 --> 00:44:11.529
topology and physics have been kind of intertwined for several decades, but

00:44:11.529 --> 00:44:16.130
number theory and physics are really starting to communicate a lot more.

00:44:16.130 --> 00:44:19.700
This article in the — in the book, “Strange Numbers Found in Particle Collisions” — is

00:44:19.700 --> 00:44:23.599
one instance of that. And that connection is — was a complete surprise, right? I

00:44:23.599 --> 00:44:26.750
think in the story you said something like it's — it's like, you know, counting

00:44:26.750 --> 00:44:30.289
grains of rice and seeing prime numbers, or something like that. Right, right. That's no reason

00:44:30.289 --> 00:44:34.160
to believe, you know, that they should be there. So now I want to turn to Robbert because

00:44:34.160 --> 00:44:37.819
you have a different — you have a column in the book where you refer to

00:44:37.819 --> 00:44:40.760
something that sounds like what Eugene Wigner was

00:44:40.760 --> 00:44:44.030
saying, but it's a little bit different. You said there appears to be an

00:44:44.030 --> 00:44:47.930
unreasonable effectiveness of quantum theory in mathematics, that somehow

00:44:47.930 --> 00:44:54.800
quantum theory is leading to new mathematics. Well, you encourage

00:44:54.800 --> 00:44:59.060
interaction between, I would say, mathematics and the sciences, and but

00:44:59.060 --> 00:45:03.500
— it's indeed a little bit of a — kind of an arms race of these two fields. So, for

00:45:03.500 --> 00:45:07.820
instance, we would say, well, math is wonderful to describe the motions of

00:45:07.820 --> 00:45:12.710
planets, etc., but Newton had to invent calculus to do this, right? The math

00:45:12.710 --> 00:45:17.090
wasn't there yet, and it was — now we see this as a standard part of mathematics,

00:45:17.090 --> 00:45:25.430
but very much inspired by — by mechanics. And so I would say, you know, it's — math is

00:45:25.430 --> 00:45:32.180
a very forceful tool for scientists, but also nature is a great mathematics

00:45:32.180 --> 00:45:36.500
teacher and teaches us mathematics in a very deep way. And I think these

00:45:36.500 --> 00:45:41.750
lessons have only just started. Now we know that, you know, nature is quantum

00:45:41.750 --> 00:45:45.200
mechanical — so that's one thing we know and it's nothing an approximation — really

00:45:45.200 --> 00:45:48.230
— it's — if you — what's the fundamental way to think about nature is quantum

00:45:48.230 --> 00:45:54.140
mechanical. And yet I think most of our geometrical concepts and — or just our all

00:45:54.140 --> 00:45:57.740
our mathematical concepts — are kind of classical nature — we think in points

00:45:57.740 --> 00:46:02.300
and lines and curves and velocity, and so all of these would make perfect sense

00:46:02.300 --> 00:46:07.490
in classical mechanics. So our math, I think, is still almost like 19th-century

00:46:07.490 --> 00:46:11.840
a little bit and we haven't fully embraced quantum mechanics. Now it turns out

00:46:11.840 --> 00:46:16.700
that certain ideas in quantum physics which are very unnatural to us human

00:46:16.700 --> 00:46:20.780
beings because we are not elementary particles — so we never are at two places at

00:46:20.780 --> 00:46:26.080
the same time, we’re never entangled with somebody else

00:46:26.080 --> 00:46:31.690
— it's — although we use these words but we are not [LAUGHTER] — technically we are not — Right.

00:46:31.690 --> 00:46:37.070
— and so we have very little intuition. You might say our brains have been shaped by

00:46:37.070 --> 00:46:42.620
basically classical mathematics. So another question is, can the quantum

00:46:42.620 --> 00:46:46.700
world be a good math teacher? And it turns out that, you know, there's certain

00:46:46.700 --> 00:46:50.210
things in quantum mechanics which are like perfect for mathematics,

00:46:50.210 --> 00:46:53.280
for modern mathematics. And modern mathematics doesn't want to look at one

00:46:53.280 --> 00:46:58.080
object — the modern style of mathematics is you look at everything, you know, every

00:46:58.080 --> 00:47:02.130
member of the family. And you don’t look at one object, but at their — the mutual

00:47:02.130 --> 00:47:07.230
relations — relations among relations, etc. You build this very kind of communal view,

00:47:07.230 --> 00:47:11.760
and actually it’s exactly what quantum mechanics does. It's — it's, you know,

00:47:11.760 --> 00:47:16.050
it's the famous sum over histories. If you go home today, you know, if you feel

00:47:16.050 --> 00:47:19.980
you take one path, but quantum mechanically you take all possible paths,

00:47:19.980 --> 00:47:24.660
each with a certain probability — very unlikely, only elementary particles

00:47:24.660 --> 00:47:28.440
have to deal with this — but actually, it turns out that also it's wonderful

00:47:28.440 --> 00:47:33.210
for mathematicians to deal with that, to kind of try to think like a

00:47:33.210 --> 00:47:39.830
quantum particle. And it — and again here the proof or the — the evidence for

00:47:39.830 --> 00:47:46.950
the usefulness of this unreasonable effectiveness of these ideas is that it

00:47:46.950 --> 00:47:51.090
actually leads to concrete proof of mathematical theorem. So there is a whole

00:47:51.090 --> 00:47:56.480
series of results proven in the last few decades — which I would say been

00:47:56.480 --> 00:48:02.760
proven or understood using quantum logic. And the remarkable thing I feel is that

00:48:02.760 --> 00:48:08.010
we are able to push our intuition, even our mathematical intuition, in an area —

00:48:08.010 --> 00:48:12.980
the world of small particles — where none of us have any concrete experience. That’s fascinating.

00:48:12.980 --> 00:48:18.450
I'm taking mental notes here, by the way, for future Quanta stories. [LAUGHTER] I mean that's

00:48:18.450 --> 00:48:21.000
such a great thing, you know. Think, for instance, of the life sciences. John, what you

00:48:21.000 --> 00:48:25.380
were saying — what would happen — literally happen — if we move math into

00:48:25.380 --> 00:48:31.050
that area which is as ill understood, so to say, and we have as little intuition

00:48:31.050 --> 00:48:36.000
as we have elementary particles. And so science can really inform new math in

00:48:36.000 --> 00:48:41.400
very deep ways. I would say the — we did a profile in the last year of a mathematician

00:48:41.400 --> 00:48:45.720
named Minhyong Kim, who is very much using some quantum ideas to try and

00:48:45.720 --> 00:48:50.849
understand the rational solutions to equations. It's hard to find the rational

00:48:50.849 --> 00:48:54.930
solutions to equations, but Kim has this idea that if you think about these

00:48:54.930 --> 00:49:00.030
spaces of spaces that Robbert was kind of just talking about, you can kind of —

00:49:00.030 --> 00:49:03.630
the rational solutions to these equations, so what you want to find, are like the

00:49:03.630 --> 00:49:07.260
classical paths traveled, and the — kind of the — all the other

00:49:07.260 --> 00:49:11.880
solutions are like the path not taken in the classical world. And an

00:49:11.880 --> 00:49:14.940
interesting thing about that article, though, was Kim has kind of harbored this

00:49:14.940 --> 00:49:20.340
idea — that these ideas from physics should inform these ideas from math — for

00:49:20.340 --> 00:49:24.270
more than a decade. And he, like, didn't want to tell anyone [LAUGHTER]. Because he was really

00:49:24.270 --> 00:49:28.260
afraid number theorists would, you know, would think it was just quackery — Yes, yes.

00:49:28.260 --> 00:49:31.380
— so he kind of worked up the courage over the course of a decade, and now was

00:49:31.380 --> 00:49:35.010
trying to, you know, get — gained some adherents. But it takes a while, I think, to

00:49:35.010 --> 00:49:38.610
kind of break out of these ways of thinking about these math — mathematical —

00:49:38.610 --> 00:49:43.170
questions and kind of admit a new perspective. And none of us would ever

00:49:43.170 --> 00:49:48.060
think of this ourselves — you can meditate for a millennium but you will

00:49:48.060 --> 00:49:51.600
never find the rules of quantum mechanics — experiment, nature

00:49:51.600 --> 00:49:57.810
forces us to think like these crazy ways. Yeah, wow. So I'm going to ask another question,

00:49:57.810 --> 00:50:06.000
but while we're talking our volunteers here will be bringing microphones up to

00:50:06.000 --> 00:50:09.720
the aisles here in case anybody from the audience has any

00:50:09.720 --> 00:50:12.960
questions. So please start making your way to the aisle if you have a question.

00:50:12.960 --> 00:50:15.870
And we're going to line up behind two microphones that they're setting up

00:50:15.870 --> 00:50:20.850
right now, as we speak, and we'll just continue on. But in a few minutes we're

00:50:20.850 --> 00:50:26.880
going to start taking questions. So, so far tonight

00:50:26.880 --> 00:50:31.500
we've talked about the nightmare scenario in fundamental particle physics,

00:50:31.500 --> 00:50:36.240
we've talked about the difficulty that we have with evidence in science and

00:50:36.240 --> 00:50:40.190
what that means, we've talked about how we don't know what time is —

00:50:40.190 --> 00:50:45.450
it's been kind of a little bit negative. [LAUGHTER] And I apologize for that — that's my fault

00:50:45.450 --> 00:50:49.530
for leading the conversation this way. But, yeah, I want to end sort of on a

00:50:49.530 --> 00:50:55.170
positive note, and thinking and asking every one of our panelists to talk about

00:50:55.170 --> 00:51:01.560
what is the most exciting thing that you're seeing in the area that you cover

00:51:01.560 --> 00:51:08.190
and write about, or research, and, you know, what is it likely to lead to, and how is

00:51:08.190 --> 00:51:11.100
it going to bear fruit in the future? So in physics, for example, what are you

00:51:11.100 --> 00:51:16.860
seeing out there that's most promising?
Well, so there is so much happening in

00:51:16.860 --> 00:51:20.540
physics and I didn't — definitely didn't mean to

00:51:20.540 --> 00:51:25.369
convey that there isn't. I think we've been talking about fundamental physics,

00:51:25.369 --> 00:51:29.869
so cosmology and particle physics, but of course there's so much happening in

00:51:29.869 --> 00:51:33.980
condensed matter physics, so — better understanding materials, and it's

00:51:33.980 --> 00:51:41.180
actually fascinating stuff happening there. And also, just, like, turbulence. So

00:51:41.180 --> 00:51:44.630
there are all kinds of different areas that aren't kind of getting at, like, the

00:51:44.630 --> 00:51:48.290
heart of what's going on with the universe. But even at — even at the

00:51:48.290 --> 00:51:53.450
larger sort of, you know, sort of larger scale. LIGO, for example, has

00:51:53.450 --> 00:51:56.990
been hugely transformative in terms of our ability to see what's out there,

00:51:56.990 --> 00:52:02.990
right? Yeah. And so we're now detecting gravitational waves from colliding black

00:52:02.990 --> 00:52:07.220
holes, and that's helping us kind of map out and understand the universe, and

00:52:07.220 --> 00:52:11.450
especially the role of black holes in the universe. But I would say, actually,

00:52:11.450 --> 00:52:19.330
what excites me most is that in this period of — kind of — turmoil and crisis,

00:52:19.330 --> 00:52:25.850
basically, in fundamental physics, there is — there are a lot of new ideas kind of

00:52:25.850 --> 00:52:30.800
bubbling up. And mostly what it is, is people are questioning the assumptions

00:52:30.800 --> 00:52:34.130
that they've held for a long time. Or they just kind of felt like we would be

00:52:34.130 --> 00:52:40.550
playing out this plan that we'd laid out of, you know, looking for supersymmetry,

00:52:40.550 --> 00:52:44.840
and finding it, and then understanding kind of how all the particles in nature

00:52:44.840 --> 00:52:49.760
fit together. And then kind of, you know — and maybe getting evidence for string

00:52:49.760 --> 00:52:53.930
theory somehow, and then we're done. And that obviously has not happened. So

00:52:53.930 --> 00:52:57.320
people are going back and really questioning the assumptions like

00:52:57.320 --> 00:53:02.180
naturalness — they're trying to better understand the patterns in the equation.

00:53:02.180 --> 00:53:09.080
So kind of mysterious things about the equations that we have already, to

00:53:09.080 --> 00:53:15.740
try to understand why they are the way they are. And I think there is just a

00:53:15.740 --> 00:53:19.640
lot of fascinating stuff coming out. Absolutely, yeah. We talked about, you know —

00:53:19.640 --> 00:53:26.000
there's this sort of, you know, moniker of the nightmare scenario out there, but

00:53:26.000 --> 00:53:28.700
but really talking to, especially, some of the younger physicists out there, they

00:53:28.700 --> 00:53:31.730
actually see a big, wide-open space. They see a lot of opportunity.

00:53:31.730 --> 00:53:35.750
They no longer have to follow their mentors into any field, they can just

00:53:35.750 --> 00:53:38.570
create their own programs now. They can think about, you know, anything that they

00:53:38.570 --> 00:53:45.470
can imagine to — to try to create new physics. What about in — in your realm,

00:53:45.470 --> 00:53:49.010
in biology? So I'm going to slightly cheat because I sort of alluded to one of

00:53:49.010 --> 00:53:52.850
these before, which is I think that, really, in terms of a big concept, the

00:53:52.850 --> 00:53:57.920
idea that embedded within nature just — just the sheer nature of the physical world

00:53:57.920 --> 00:54:01.730
is such that it can drive a lot of the
form and what we find in nature so there

00:54:01.730 --> 00:54:05.150
there are scientists like Jeremy England at M.I.T., for example, who've been

00:54:05.150 --> 00:54:09.200
developing arguments that suggest that there's a kind of inevitability to life

00:54:09.200 --> 00:54:13.580
because life is a natural manifestation of a tendency in the material world to

00:54:13.580 --> 00:54:19.310
keep finding better and better ways to dissipate entropy. So just — so I think

00:54:19.310 --> 00:54:23.030
that that is kind of a fascinating idea, that way. That basically that life, that —

00:54:23.030 --> 00:54:27.650
there's an inevitability to life that way, and just that the complexity that we

00:54:27.650 --> 00:54:31.010
see arising in life over time, that there might actually be an innate

00:54:31.010 --> 00:54:34.730
directionality toward more complexity that comes to that. But even beyond that

00:54:34.730 --> 00:54:38.270
—I mean, just the thing that I think is just endlessly fascinating is all the

00:54:38.270 --> 00:54:41.720
stuff that we — you would think we would understand about life by now, how often

00:54:41.720 --> 00:54:45.740
we really don't. You know, Craig Venter in his group a few years ago, they said

00:54:45.740 --> 00:54:49.430
let's take all that we understand about genomics and let's try to create

00:54:49.430 --> 00:54:53.390
the simplest living cell we possibly could. And so they took all the kind of

00:54:53.390 --> 00:54:56.330
things and they tried to figure out what would be the minimum number of cells — of

00:54:56.330 --> 00:55:00.140
genes they could put into an artificial cell. And they tried and tried and tried,

00:55:00.140 --> 00:55:03.619
and it was a dismal failure because they couldn't keep those things alive. So they

00:55:03.619 --> 00:55:06.800
tried a different way, where they started off with a really small cell and then

00:55:06.800 --> 00:55:10.490
just started randomly knocking things out to see what would be left. And what

00:55:10.490 --> 00:55:16.000
they were left in the end was this artificial organism that had only,

00:55:16.000 --> 00:55:22.070
like, 473 genes, I think, but what — which you know really, really small, it's like a

00:55:22.070 --> 00:55:25.609
10th the size of what you'd find in something like an E. coli. But here's the

00:55:25.609 --> 00:55:33.020
kicker: Of those 473, like 150 of them — we have no idea what those genes do. So even

00:55:33.020 --> 00:55:36.380
when we tried to get it down to the minimum number of stuff, we found that we

00:55:36.380 --> 00:55:42.020
still don't even understand why those things were alive. And so I — there's so

00:55:42.020 --> 00:55:44.770
much still to just learn at that basic level of what our

00:55:44.770 --> 00:55:48.880
genome is doing. A lot to learn, yes. Kevin?
Yeah. I think there's, I mean, a lot of

00:55:48.880 --> 00:55:52.690
exciting work going on in math. I mentioned the search for rational

00:55:52.690 --> 00:55:57.130
solutions to polynomial equations. There's — there’s like several kind of

00:55:57.130 --> 00:56:00.910
exciting initiatives in that respect. One mathematician recently — I know — I

00:56:00.910 --> 00:56:04.840
think that mathematicians are optimistic — that we will be kind of identifying rational

00:56:04.840 --> 00:56:10.870
solutions in a more systematic way. I think kind of a work that, kind of, puts

00:56:10.870 --> 00:56:14.080
analysis and algebra in conversation with each other and allows

00:56:14.080 --> 00:56:17.950
mathematicians to kind of port results back and forth, there's like a lot of exciting

00:56:17.950 --> 00:56:23.920
work there. And that opens up kind of solutions on both sides. Yeah, those are

00:56:23.920 --> 00:56:27.100
definitely true. Certainly a lot that's going on in mathematics. And things are —

00:56:27.100 --> 00:56:32.110
maybe it's harder to talk about them all as one thing, but that's — those are

00:56:32.110 --> 00:56:35.680
great examples. Thank you, Kevin. And what about you, Robbert?

00:56:35.680 --> 00:56:39.320
I would highlight the, kind of the, collision of the small and the

00:56:39.660 --> 00:56:44.740
very large, so where cosmology and quantum theory are colliding, you know.

00:56:44.740 --> 00:56:50.770
I think we all are excited about what we do not understand, so it's, I think

00:56:50.770 --> 00:56:54.460
most of you know, 95 percent of the universe is ill-understood. It's dark

00:56:54.460 --> 00:56:58.300
matter, dark energy. We know there are black holes, we know there’s something like the

00:56:58.300 --> 00:57:03.220
Big Bang, we know that nature found a way to resolve the logical problems, so these

00:57:03.220 --> 00:57:07.810
are I think often said these are the
paradoxes of the 21st century as the

00:57:07.810 --> 00:57:12.550
atom was the paradox for the 20th century. So though they’re out there somehow,

00:57:12.550 --> 00:57:16.840
nature found a way around, and what I find extremely exciting for theoretical

00:57:16.840 --> 00:57:21.580
physics and mathematics, actually, I feel it's like a jigsaw puzzle. We started on

00:57:21.580 --> 00:57:26.110
the very edge, the easy parts, perhaps the corners, and now we're going into the

00:57:26.110 --> 00:57:31.210
middle part where you actually see ideas for many, many fields coming together. So

00:57:31.210 --> 00:57:36.700
when you study gravity or quantum physics or quantum information or very

00:57:36.700 --> 00:57:40.660
pure math, perhaps even number theory, somehow it all comes together. So if

00:57:40.660 --> 00:57:45.220
you're a young person right now, you can kind of have this great pleasure of

00:57:45.220 --> 00:57:49.330
moving in all these worlds and still thinking about the same problem. So, I

00:57:49.330 --> 00:57:53.800
think that's actually extremely exciting, and I think that actually makes — I think

00:57:53.800 --> 00:57:57.970
like in science you can say every year is the

00:57:57.970 --> 00:58:01.119
most exciting year ever [LAUGHTER] —

00:58:01.119 --> 00:58:07.599
I think you have been right for every year. So I took you sort of down, you know, a little bit at first,

00:58:07.599 --> 00:58:12.099
but we ended on a positive note. I want to take some questions now from the

00:58:12.099 --> 00:58:15.539
audience. I think you were first, sir, on this side.

00:58:15.539 --> 00:58:17.530
There are two outstanding

00:58:17.530 --> 00:58:21.280
mysteries in quantum mechanics that you
haven't touched on tonight so I'd like

00:58:21.280 --> 00:58:27.369
o get your opinion. The one I'm not really asking your opinion on is: What

00:58:27.369 --> 00:58:30.220
does quantum mechanics really tell us about the nature of the universe?

00:58:30.220 --> 00:58:33.910
I fear that Einstein was wrong and God does throw dice, but that's not my question.

00:58:33.910 --> 00:58:37.000
Another outstanding problem is the

00:58:37.000 --> 00:58:42.579
problem of measurement that you all know about. And John Bell, probably close to

00:58:42.579 --> 00:58:46.030
his death, wrote an essay called “On Measurement” in which he challenged

00:58:46.030 --> 00:58:50.559
quantum physicists to incorporate measurement into quantum theory, and I'd

00:58:50.559 --> 00:58:56.740
like your comments by any of you on how much success, how — how things are

00:58:56.740 --> 00:59:07.030
going on that course. So yeah, I've written a lot about interpretive issues with

00:59:07.030 --> 00:59:11.530
quantum mechanics, basically the question is what — what does it mean, what

00:59:11.530 --> 00:59:16.780
is reality if things are so probabilistic on the quantum scale —

00:59:16.780 --> 00:59:21.670
nothing's real until you measure it, you know — it brings up all these questions. I

00:59:21.670 --> 00:59:29.530
tend to think that that — we just, that — in the next revolution in physics, when w

00:59:29.530 --> 00:59:34.140
have a better, deeper understanding, that it will be clear that our current

00:59:34.140 --> 00:59:40.180
thoughts on this issue are naïve. And that we will no longer be,

00:59:40.180 --> 00:59:44.589
we won't — it won't be that we turned — it turned out that a single interpretation

00:59:44.589 --> 00:59:50.819
was right and the other ones weren't. It'll be just unimaginably different.

00:59:51.270 --> 01:00:06.160
Thank you, and [UNINTELLIGIBLE] I think we should probably also allow others to ask [UNINTELLIGIBLE] — and I

01:00:06.160 --> 01:00:10.240
just want to say real quick, that was a great question, and please do make sure

01:00:10.240 --> 01:00:13.320
your questions are questions and
in brief so that we can get a few more

01:00:13.320 --> 01:00:20.700
questions in today. So let's start on this side. This is not exactly a specific

01:00:20.700 --> 01:00:27.360
question, but if you could comment on the statement: Is it meaningful and what does

01:00:27.360 --> 01:00:34.880
it mean for an axiom to be true? That's somewhat of — Mathematics — Yeah.

01:00:34.880 --> 01:00:38.790
It’s mathematical and philosophical, but I think you guys could have some good

01:00:38.790 --> 01:00:45.640
answers or comments. Thanks for that 
 deep question. Kevin? You’re not looking at me, are you?

01:00:45.880 --> 01:00:48.880
You are our math panelist —

01:00:48.900 --> 01:00:54.440
What does it mean for an axiom to be true? I don’t know. I’m afraid I don’t know. [LAUGHTER]

01:00:55.220 --> 01:00:56.220
I mean, those are things that are often

01:00:56.360 --> 01:01:02.460
based in physics, right? Like we — we start with axioms in math that seem true, based

01:01:02.460 --> 01:01:06.960
on our understanding. This relates directly to this point of math

01:01:06.960 --> 01:01:11.070
being discovered or invented, so we think often, you know, if it's natural

01:01:11.070 --> 01:01:14.910
mathematical structures they are discovered. So somebody finds like

01:01:14.910 --> 01:01:19.770
symmetry groups, you know, oh, OK, that's a natural thing. But you can have very contrived

01:01:19.770 --> 01:01:25.560
actions, and then you have a creative world. Well, clearly you can discover

01:01:25.560 --> 01:01:30.450
discover things in that world, but a whole world seems to be kind of made-up, right? So the

01:01:30.450 --> 01:01:34.920
question is, what are in some sense the natural — I mean that's how I read — what

01:01:34.920 --> 01:01:39.530
are the natural actions? And here you might think that, no, perhaps indeed our

01:01:39.530 --> 01:01:46.530
science/physics/reality experiences are guiding us because we do not want to

01:01:46.530 --> 01:01:51.620
have them completely — now you can have very sterile or very contrived

01:01:51.620 --> 01:02:00.230
mathematical worlds that feel made-up. There's even the incompleteness theorem — there are things that can sort of

01:02:00.230 --> 01:02:05.930
undermine your attempts to find out, you know, sort of the fundamental axioms. This side, please.

01:02:05.930 --> 01:02:18.050
In my inbox the Quanta Magazine has a soft little sibling called Nautilus, and

01:02:18.050 --> 01:02:23.310
recently there was an article in Nautilus about quantum — quantum

01:02:23.310 --> 01:02:29.270
entanglement and consciousness. Penrose apparently has been dabbling in

01:02:29.270 --> 01:02:35.000
this and looking at microelements of the brain — specifically, I believe, it was

01:02:35.000 --> 01:02:40.520
something like microtubules. So I was wondering whether that's a question that

01:02:40.520 --> 01:02:44.960
you all have encountered. It sounded like a fair amount of the world dismisses it

01:02:44.960 --> 01:02:50.150
out of hand, and then there are a couple of people who think that he — well, maybe

01:02:50.150 --> 01:02:55.880
there is something there. So I was curious to hear whether anyone had any

01:02:55.880 --> 01:03:04.760
thoughts on this. And then in particular relation to another, I think growing,

01:03:04.760 --> 01:03:11.390
tradition of thought around a much more decentralized architecture of the human

01:03:11.390 --> 01:03:16.760
neural system. So it's not just the brain, because in this case you had quantum

01:03:16.760 --> 01:03:23.059
entanglement with elementary or microelements of the brain, but if you have  a

01:03:23.059 --> 01:03:29.150
larger system that includes the gut and the toes and so on and so forth. So any

01:03:29.150 --> 01:03:34.640
thoughts? I'll jump in at least sort of briefly on a couple of those. Obviously,

01:03:34.640 --> 01:03:43.579
one dismisses Roger Penrose casually at one's peril. That said, I think

01:03:43.579 --> 01:03:48.950
you're right in saying that I think that the view of most neuroscientists is that

01:03:48.950 --> 01:03:54.980
an idea of trying to pin notions of consciousness, and where that comes down

01:03:54.980 --> 01:03:59.000
to some sort of quantum effects associated specifically with

01:03:59.000 --> 01:04:04.880
microtubules, seems questionable for a variety of different reasons that I am

01:04:04.880 --> 01:04:07.940
certainly not capable of being able to say at the moment. But I know that is

01:04:07.940 --> 01:04:12.230
general state of it, I think. As with all these sorts of issues, the right

01:04:12.230 --> 01:04:15.529
experiments coming along could easily change opinions about that. Because it's

01:04:15.529 --> 01:04:18.799
not as though we're — have all sorts of other great ideas right now about where

01:04:18.799 --> 01:04:22.819
consciousness is coming from, but I don't think that's a very favored explanation

01:04:22.819 --> 01:04:29.329
right now. I think that there — I think your other comment about the sort of, the

01:04:29.329 --> 01:04:33.619
decentralization of a lot of functions that we've tended to associate with the

01:04:33.619 --> 01:04:38.660
brain itself, I think that is a very active area of research. Although I think

01:04:38.660 --> 01:04:42.500
my own reading of the literature on that is that most of that has less to do with

01:04:42.500 --> 01:04:46.609
the notion — with actual quantum effects — and more of a growing sense that in fact —

01:04:46.609 --> 01:04:50.869
that a lot of things that we thought that had to be just purely localized

01:04:50.869 --> 01:04:55.039
inside the brain can in fact be distributed more outside to other parts

01:04:55.039 --> 01:04:58.430
of the nervous system because that information can be communicated and

01:04:58.430 --> 01:05:01.910
shared in other ways. That's a sort of quick gloss on that, anyway. Thank you

01:05:01.910 --> 01:05:06.380
John, and because we started a little bit late we're going — I'll take two more

01:05:06.380 --> 01:05:10.490
questions, and then afterwards we're going to have a little bit of a reception.

01:05:10.490 --> 01:05:13.400
There'll be some refreshments outside, we'll do a book signing for those of you

01:05:13.400 --> 01:05:16.400
who have books, and so you'll have an opportunity to talk with panelists

01:05:16.400 --> 01:05:19.190
individually and ask your questions there. So we'll take just two more questions.

01:05:21.339 --> 01:05:29.029
Humankind has needed to find beauty in reality, and we see this in mathematics,

01:05:29.029 --> 01:05:35.170
in physics, and any geometrical explanation, interpretation of nature

01:05:35.170 --> 01:05:42.349
leads to certain beauty. Just think of symmetry, Fibonacci series, etc. etc. There

01:05:42.349 --> 01:05:48.140
is a long tradition. Can you speak of an aesthetics of

01:05:48.140 --> 01:05:50.380
cosmology — is there such a thing?

01:05:50.380 --> 01:05:54.700
That’s an interesting question. Want to give that a shot, Robbert?

01:05:54.960 --> 01:05:59.420
Well, I often remark that beauty is becoming

01:05:59.420 --> 01:06:04.940
something very contentious in the arts, but in some sense it's now

01:06:04.940 --> 01:06:13.490
migrating to science. [LAUGHTER] So we are the ultimate refuge of beauty. [LAUGHTER] And — and if — I think, you know,

01:06:13.490 --> 01:06:18.380
somehow our definition of so — it's quite remarkable that these equations and

01:06:18.380 --> 01:06:21.970
objects and that we have a universal sense of beauty, you know.

01:06:21.970 --> 01:06:25.789
Mathematicians from all different backgrounds agree, this is a beautiful

01:06:25.789 --> 01:06:29.690
equation. I don't think there's a real good definition of it, but has something

01:06:29.690 --> 01:06:34.970
to do, I would almost say, of the power per symbol of the equation. So the fac

01:06:34.970 --> 01:06:40.880
that you, with a very brief set of concepts, can explain a lot. And so I

01:06:40.880 --> 01:06:47.480
would say, if you look at the great beauty I see in physics now — and I've

01:06:47.480 --> 01:06:51.740
written about it in one column in the physics collection — you find it at the

01:06:51.740 --> 01:06:54.270
two ends of physics— at the very small and very

01:06:54.270 --> 01:06:59.220
large, where things start to simplify, basically because you either zoom in so

01:06:59.220 --> 01:07:03.390
closely that you don't see any of the messy details, or you zoom out so largely

01:07:03.390 --> 01:07:08.250
you only see the big picture. And in these two areas, I think the equations, say

01:07:08.250 --> 01:07:12.000
Einstein's equations or the equations of the standard model, are incredibly

01:07:12.000 --> 01:07:18.270
beautiful because they are so powerful. So just the fact that with a very simple

01:07:18.270 --> 01:07:24.090
set of equations for describing 14 billion years of evolution, again, the

01:07:24.090 --> 01:07:29.070
surprise — again — has been that people think of all kind of bells and

01:07:29.070 --> 01:07:31.950
whistles to add to it, and nature proves them wrong.

01:07:31.950 --> 01:07:37.950
So there is something — I find it very comforting that, you know — I

01:07:37.950 --> 01:07:42.480
think we're trying to read the character of nature, and think what she likes

01:07:42.480 --> 01:07:47.640
and I think it's the kind of equations that we like, too. On the subject of

01:07:47.640 --> 01:07:51.840
mathematical beauty, I mean, for anybody who hasn't heard of this, a number of

01:07:51.840 --> 01:07:55.230
years ago the mathematician Michael Atiyah and some neuroscientists did a

01:07:55.230 --> 01:08:00.930
paper where they looked at fMRIs or something of mathematicians’

01:08:00.930 --> 01:08:03.780
brains as they were looking at different

01:08:03.780 --> 01:08:09.210
equations to see which patterns matched the patterns of other people looking at

01:08:09.210 --> 01:08:14.340
beautiful art and sort of the aesthetics of that. And they found that the one

01:08:14.340 --> 01:08:18.330
equation that most mathematicians found to be the most beautiful was Euler’s

01:08:18.330 --> 01:08:23.880
equation, Euler’s identity — eiπ + 1 = 0 — and it does

01:08:23.880 --> 01:08:27.390
embody all the things that Robbert was just talking about. One last question.

01:08:27.390 --> 01:08:34.550
Hi. So Robbert’s comments on quantum mechanics being a mathematical teacher

01:08:34.550 --> 01:08:42.480
made me think about, you know, the recent computing systems involving the

01:08:42.480 --> 01:08:48.090
blockchain and decentralized computing, how that's pushed sort of the frontiers

01:08:48.090 --> 01:08:56.790
of cryptography and a lot of other stuff. And so I wonder, first of all, when do you

01:08:56.790 --> 01:09:05.789
think the first hobby quantum computer will be available? [LAUGHTER] We’re giving one away tonight actually — [LAUGHTER]

01:09:05.789 --> 01:09:13.440
And what questions and — and topics, you know, do you think, you know, could be addressed in terms of

01:09:13.440 --> 01:09:18.750
quantum mechanics and mathematics that
this potential computer would sort of

01:09:18.750 --> 01:09:27.100
spur? They always say 20 years —  and then in 20 years it would be another 20 years, of course.

01:09:27.580 --> 01:09:37.340
I can tell you exactly when it will be available, but not where. [LAUGHTER]

01:09:37.349 --> 01:09:42.690
I don't know if we can give you a better answer than that to be honest. [LAUGHTER] Thank you everyone, and again we will

01:09:42.690 --> 01:09:45.599
have some refreshments outside a little
bit of a reception and if you have books

01:09:45.599 --> 01:09:48.299
you'd like us to sign, the panelists — except for Robbert, I think you have to leave?

01:09:48.299 --> 01:09:51.809
Unfortunately, we won't have Robbert — but we thank you for being here

01:09:51.809 --> 01:09:56.099
and for your amazing insights that you shared with us, and all the panelists today. Thank you.

01:09:56.160 --> 01:10:00.380
[APPLAUSE]

01:10:02.440 --> 01:10:59.420
